  "answer": "Mitosis and meiosis are both types of cell division, but they differ in several key aspects. Mitosis is a process where a single cell divides into two identical daughter cells, and it occurs in most body cells. It maintains the same number of chromosomes in the daughter cells as the parent cell. Meiosis, on the other hand, is a specialized type of cell division that occurs only in the formation of gametes (sperm and eggs) during sexual reproduction. It results in four daughter cells, each with half the number of chromosomes of the parent cell. This is why meiosis is also called reduction division. Additionally, meiosis involves two stages (meiosis I and meiosis II), while mitosis involves only one stage. In mitosis, the chromosomes are not paired, whereas in meiosis, homologous chromosomes pair up and exchange genetic material during prophase I. These differences ensure genetic diversity in offspring during sexual reproduction.",
